Do not apply a version-1 save file's levels, object bit and attributes to symbols or R_NilValue.

NewReadItem() resolves a SYMSXP item to the interned symbol from the
file's symbol table (index 0, or an unrecognized primitive, to
R_NilValue) and then applies the item's LEVELS, OBJECT bit and attribute
list to that shared object, exactly as it does for a freshly allocated
one. A well-formed 30-byte RDA1 file therefore leaves an interned symbol
with attributes and the object bit set ("****found a symbol with
attributes" at every later garbage collection), makes it evaluate as a
..N reference (levels bit 1) or bypass the environment chain (base
symbol cache bit), or puts attributes and the object bit on NULL itself,
for the rest of the session.

The writer emits an attribute item for every object, so the reader still
consumes it for these cases but leaves the object alone.

The upstream PR also adds a regression test (tests/reg-tests-1e.R), not
carried here.

Found while triaging the OSS-Fuzz aftermath crashers 6196191659098112
and 4716725078392832 (afl_r_load), whose logs carry the symbol warning.

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #399, filed 2026-10-23.
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk.

--- a/src/main/saveload.c
+++ b/src/main/saveload.c
@@ -1300,6 +1300,16 @@
     default:
 	error(_("NewReadItem: unknown type %i"), type);
     }
+    /* A symbol item resolves to the interned symbol, and an index of 0
+       (or an unrecognized primitive) to R_NilValue: both are shared, so
+       the levels, object bit and attributes in the file must not be
+       applied to them.  The writer still emits an attribute item for
+       every object, so read it to stay in step with the stream. */
+    if (TYPEOF(s) == SYMSXP || s == R_NilValue) {
+	NewReadItem(sym_table, env_table, fp, m, d);
+	UNPROTECT(1); /* s */
+	return s;
+    }
     SETLEVELS(s, (unsigned short) levs);
     SET_OBJECT(s, objf);
     SET_ATTRIB(s, NewReadItem(sym_table, env_table, fp, m, d));
